import csv,json,hashlib
from pathlib import Path
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;R=B.parent
rows=list(csv.DictReader((B/'queue.csv').open(encoding='utf8',newline='')))
D={
'Q071':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','The 16 retained dip flags in S18/S58/S85/S86 trace subday irregular troughs, waves and segment boundaries; S18 1806.72 has only 43% of nominal core cadence coverage and S86 3640.23 lies by a gap. Representative raw SAP does not preserve a repeated ingress–flat core–egress profile or a coherent phase. KSPSAP comparison at flags likewise gives no common eclipse morphology.','This refutes the selected repeat-sector eclipse interpretation, not all possible low-level variability.','A clean isolated dip with a stable baseline and repeated same-kind timing would reopen the target.'),
'Q072':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','All seven selected sectors show large smooth, changing waves and segment offsets. The S56 2837.98 dip is a descending wave near a gap; S75 flagged dips are broad wave shoulders, and S82/S83 flags sit on extraction/segment behavior. The raw context has no repeated isolated eclipse shape on the Gaia 34.594-d clock. Exact-target VSX V0602 Cyg is classed SRD at 34.5 d, supporting a non-eclipse variable interpretation but not establishing a mechanism from this light curve.','The selected eclipse evidence is refuted; the star can remain a genuine non-eclipse variable.','Use independent high-cadence photometry if a narrow eclipse under the large waves is suspected.'),
'Q073':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','Only S54 and S81 are cached. S54 2784.35 sits on a long recovery/ramp after a discontinuity; S81 3518.90 coincides with a large gap/step, while the other two flags are trend/step features. None provides a closed eclipse in raw SAP; two flagged sectors therefore do not demonstrate recurrence.','The positive step/gap evidence refutes the selected eclipse windows.','Require a complete symmetric event independent of the segment steps.'),
'Q074':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','The two retained flags, S94 3874.88 and S102 4145.07, cover only 28% and 46% of nominal cores respectively. S94 is a sharp loss/recovery adjacent to a cadence gap and S102 a discontinuity near another gap; other sectors show weak long trends. No physical center or repeated profile is measured.','The identified detector-edge windows are refuted as evidence of an eclipse; weak unrelated events are not excluded.','Obtain a complete event with covered core, both baselines and target-centered pixel loss.'),
'Q075':('PLAUSIBLE','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','S99 has two broad raw SAP V-shaped depressions at about 4053.25 and 4068.06 BTJD, 14.81 d apart, with good FFI core/comparison counts. A MAST TIC cone finds a 3.51-times-target T-band source at 49 arcsec, WCS (6.93,2.98), near both difference peaks (6,3), plus a 0.37-times-target neighbor at 19.7 arcsec; these are capable alternatives. S101 has a further dip near 4120.04 but its center is split by a gap; S10 flags are much weaker in KSPSAP. The two clean S99 events are the same observing sector, and no unique integer-cycle clock is established across S101. Helper difference images of the S99 pair both peak one to two pixels below the target, with centroids about one pixel away, so source ownership remains open.','These could be eclipses from a nearby source or structured/systematic variability; same-sector recurrence and fractional-pixel centroids do not settle that.','Resolve the bright TIC neighbors with higher-resolution time-series photometry and obtain a second-sector target-centered difference image at a predicted same-kind event.'),
'Q076':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','Across 43 cached sectors only three dip flags remain. S87 3675.34 is a shallow broad undulation, S94 3874.85 has 28% nominal core coverage beside a gap, and S95 3889.01 is a broad step/trend. The other sectors show variable slopes and edges without a stable repeated eclipse profile. S94 is nearly simultaneous with Q074 S94 3874.88, a common-timing clue rather than source proof.','The selected flags do not establish a binary eclipse.','Seek complete raw dips in independent sectors, then test source pixels and aliases.'),
'Q077':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','Raw S82 has 39 deep minima at BTJD 3533.807–3558.17 with a same-kind interval 0.641193 d and alternating shallow minima halfway between; S14/S40/S54/S74 show the same sharp morphology. SAP, DET_FLUX and SYS_RM_FLUX retain the pattern. Yet TESSCut S14 eclipse difference peaks at pixel (4,4), 2.54 pixels from the target WCS (5.84,5.81). Gaia 2126300770519521024, G=12.12, lies 5.05 arcsec from the loss centroid and is capable; the target is 52.33 arcsec away. VSX ASASSN-V J193328.10+440008.5 is an EW with P=0.641235 d at the displaced neighbor position. This is a real off-target EB lead, not evidence that the queued Gaia 30.03-d SB1 eclipses.','The target may be binary spectroscopically; this refutes assignment of the short-period eclipses to it.','Preserve the neighbor as a separate known EB and seek target-only photometry for the 30.03-d orbit.'),
'Q078':('PROBABLE','COMPLETE','UNKNOWN','','','','S99 shows eleven narrow V-shaped raw minima across consecutive ~0.693052-d intervals (4.7-minute RMS of minimum times), with similar recurring V morphology in earlier and later sectors. SAP, DET_FLUX and SYS_RM_FLUX preserve the S99 signal. TESSCut S11 difference centroid is 0.39 pixel from the target WCS, but a TIC neighbor at 13.1 arcsec and T-band flux ratio 0.050 is only 0.30 pixel from that centroid; another at 22.7 arcsec has ratio 0.34. The 4-ppt signal does not uniquely belong to the target. The Gaia 57.029-d SB1 period is not the measured short photometric clock. VSX has an exact-position GDS variable without EB type, plus more distant Gaia E entries; none alone establishes an exact-target known EB.','A repeated eclipse-like signal is real, but close-source ownership and orbital-cycle/harmonic interpretation remain unresolved.','Use higher-resolution or multi-aperture eclipse localization to separate the target from capable neighbors.'),
'Q079':('CONFIRMED','COMPLETE','KNOWN','28.3427','','1379.834','Twelve raw flat-bottomed eclipses from S2 through S96 fit 1379.83394+28.34273608*n BTJD, n=0–90, with 0.0585-d center RMS. Consecutive n=62,63,64 and n=88,89,90 break sparse long-baseline aliases. Local trapezoids are ~1.4–2.0 d wide and ~26–43 ppt deep; independent sector overlays preserve ingress/flat core/egress. SYS_RM_FLUX retains the S67 36.5-ppt dip while DET_FLUX suppresses it, a detrending limitation rather than a raw refutation. S13 TESSCut loss centroid is 0.064 pixel from target WCS; exact-position VSX ASAS J030241-7332.9 is ESD|EC, catalog period 28.27 d. Gaia SB1 P=28.341827 d agrees; nominal edge-on conjunction is 0.10 d from S13 measured center, subject to correlated orbital uncertainties. Fully covered half-phase windows in S63/S94/S96 have broad ~12-18 ppt minima, unlike the ~35-ppt flat-bottom primary; the 14.17-d identical-event alias fails this morphology test. A 56.69-d alternating-kind photometric interpretation is not excluded by primary times alone, but is disfavored by the Gaia spectroscopic period.','The Gaia neighbor cone timed out, but a MAST TIC cone finds the nearest catalogued sources at 24.0/25.2 arcsec with T-band flux ratios 0.0004/0.0007, incapable of a ~35-ppt target-aperture loss even at full disappearance. Unresolved sources remain a small residual caveat. The fitted period has no formal error because event shapes and baseline differ by sector.','Refine orbital geometry jointly with radial velocities and obtain high-resolution eclipse photometry to tighten source ownership.'),
'Q080':('PROBABLE','COMPLETE','KNOWN','','','','Measured S7 and S34 raw SAP centers near 1501.440 and 2235.630 BTJD anchor two broad troughs with covered cores and visible ingress/egress. The S87 3670.09 and S88 3703.56 screening windows show further broad depressions on a repeated ~33.38-d branch; S33 2218.97 and S87 3687.31 may belong to the opposite branch. The S7–S34 separation of ~734.19 d is compatible with 22 Gaia 33.3822-d cycles or 11 cycles at twice that period, but S87/S88 are affected by gaps or strong waves and KSPSAP suppresses the broad raw structure. S7 and S34 have continuously sampled troughs, and independent 3x3 sky-subtracted FFI reductions preserve 8.58 and 5.52 ppt losses. Their loss centroids lie within about half a pixel of the target; capable Gaia neighbors are ~2.5–2.8 pixels from the difference centroids. VSX ASAS J071145-2226.6 is an exact-position ESD|ED EB with historical P65.8 d, which does not resolve the raw cycle count or confirm each window.','S7/S34 centers are approximate local fits, with shape and baseline systematics rather than formal timing errors. S87 ingress and S88 egress are gapped, and their timing cannot resolve P versus 2P. The VSX 65.8-d and Gaia 33.38-d clocks differ, and an orbital harmonic remains possible. Difference images are not a formal PRF fit.','Fit complete target-centered eclipses over independent sectors and test both 33.38-d and 65.8-d alias families.'),
}
for r in rows:
 sid=r['sample_id'];tic=r['tic'];v,comp,nov,p,pe,epoch,body,lim,nxt=D[sid]
 f=B/tic/'REPORT.md';ctx=json.loads((B/tic/'lead-context.json').read_text());secs=','.join(str(s['sector']) for s in ctx['sectors']);
 cat=R/'tap-recovery'/f'vsx-{sid}.json';catline=f'VSX direct receipt: `{cat}` (queried 2026-11-05; exact identity assessed separately from physical verdict).'
 text=f'# {sid} TIC {tic}\n\nPhysical verdict: **{v}**. Review completion: **{comp}**. EB novelty: **{nov}**.\n\n{body}\n\nThe raw SAP sector plot is `lead-raw-context.png`; every selected dip has raw cadence values in `lead-local-series.json` and actual endpoints/gaps in `lead-flag-diagnostics.json`. Source file hashes are in `../LEAD-SOURCE-HASHES.json`; sectors reviewed: {secs}. The screening centers are predictions, not fitted centers. {catline}\n\nLimitations: {lim}\n\nNext check: {nxt}\n'
 f.write_text(text,encoding='utf8')
fields=['sample_id','tic','sample_stratum','physical_verdict','full_review_completion','novelty_status','period_days','period_error_days','primary_epoch_btjd','depth_ppt','limitations','next_check','report']
with (B/'RESULTS.csv').open('w',encoding='utf8',newline='') as h:
 w=csv.DictWriter(h,fieldnames=fields);w.writeheader()
 for r in rows:
  sid=r['sample_id'];tic=r['tic'];v,comp,nov,p,pe,epoch,body,lim,nxt=D[sid]
  w.writerow(dict(sample_id=sid,tic=tic,sample_stratum=r['sample_stratum'],physical_verdict=v,full_review_completion=comp,novelty_status=nov,period_days=p,period_error_days=pe,primary_epoch_btjd=epoch,depth_ppt='35' if sid=='Q079' else '',limitations=lim,next_check=nxt,report=str(B/tic/'REPORT.md')))
print('wrote',len(rows),'reports')


